WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.507
This query first creates a CTE named older patients.

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:06.997
That stage contains one row per patient older than fifty.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:11.547
The final query left joins admissions and counts admissions per older patient.

00:00:11.547 --> 00:00:15.012
Run it and identify the grain of each stage.

00:00:15.012 --> 00:00:22.042
The CTE is patient grain, and the final grouped result is also one row per patient.

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:26.197
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.197 --> 00:00:32.567
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.007
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:41.612
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:48.372
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:51.382
before discussing syntax.

00:00:51.382 --> 00:00:56.876
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
